{"key":"apcalcab-mcq-028","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"D","final_answer":"If a function is differentiable at x=a, then it is continuous there.","working":"Differentiability implies continuity: if f'(a) = lim(x->a) [f(x)-f(a)]/(x-a) exists, then lim(x->a) [f(x)-f(a)] = lim(x->a) [(f(x)-f(a))/(x-a)] * (x-a) = f'(a) * 0 = 0, so lim(x->a) f(x) = f(a). Thus D is always true. A is false because |x| is continuous at 0 but not differentiable. B is false because f(x)=x^3 has derivative 0 at 0 but no local extremum. C is false because differentiability requires continuity.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":496,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":240,"cacheReadTokens":256},"outputTokens":357,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":186,"reasoningTokens":171},"totalTokens":853,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":496,"completion_tokens":357,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":256,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":240,"total_tokens":853,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":256},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":171}},"reasoningTokens":171,"cachedInputTokens":256},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":3947,"keyed_label":"D"}
